WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.068
-Our next guest is
an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.535
and Tony-nominated actor

00:00:03.535 --> 00:00:06.606
you know from his work
in "Bridesmaids," "Moone Boy,"

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:07.807
and "The IT Crowd."

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.208
He stars in
"The Big Door Prize,"

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.110
which premieres tomorrow
on Apple TV+.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.778
Let's take a look.
-Believe it or not,

00:00:12.778 --> 00:00:14.380
for the two bucks
you saved on coffee,

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.618
that thing will tell you
your life's potential.

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:20.486
-Is that right? [ Chuckles ]

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:22.755
Where'd you get it?
-No idea.

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:25.658
Jacob was manning the store
when it arrived on Friday,

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:28.361
but a lot of people
used it over the weekend

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.697
and, uh, seems to be good
for business,

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.232
so I'm not asking questions.

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:35.068
-[ Chuckles ]

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:37.704
-Please welcome back to the show
Chris O'Dowd, everybody!

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:40.606
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:51.918 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.419
Welcome back! It's been a while.

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:55.588
-I know. It's been
like five years or something.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:57.457
-Five years.
I'm thrilled to have you back.

00:00:57.457 --> 00:01:00.059
This is a wonderful show
that's based on a book

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:01.060
of the same name.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.461
A machine,
as we saw in the clip,

00:01:02.461 --> 00:01:03.863
arrives in a small town.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:04.998
Lay out the premise
for us a little bit.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:06.866
-That's it.
Like, I play a...

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:08.067
what do you call it?

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.970
A high school history teacher
called Dusty.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.105
Very settled, very happy
kind of implant

00:01:13.105 --> 00:01:14.374
from Ireland, maybe 20 years.

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:15.942
But now this chapter
in his life,

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:17.110
he's married, he's got a kid.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.045
He thinks he has it sorted.

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:22.815
This machine arrives
in a local kind of grocery store

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.183
with the promise
of telling everybody

00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:26.352
what their true life
potential is.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:28.822
So, you kind of sit
into the machine

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:32.691
and it takes your fingerprints
and your Social Security number,

00:01:32.691 --> 00:01:34.227
at which point I'm out, right?
I'm out.

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:37.230
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-But this idiot...

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.999
And then it takes
a few more details about you,

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:42.068
and everybody
in the course of the story

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:44.069
and every -- all of
the inhabitants of this town

00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:45.405
start getting these cards with,

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:50.243
like, whatever --
karate teacher or whatnot --

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.346
and it starts changing
the course of their lives.

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:56.483
Like, the school principal gets
"biker" and she gets a bike.

00:01:56.483 --> 00:02:00.119
And so it's this kind of idea
that the collective delusion

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:03.656
that if you want the oracle
to give you information,

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.959
then it will
and you can follow it as much

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.193
or as little as you want to,

00:02:08.193 --> 00:02:12.164
like a football team
or a religion or -- or whatnot.

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:13.733
And so we see
all of these people.

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:15.235
This catalyst is kind of used

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:16.602
for everybody
to change their lives.

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:20.439
-And were you drawn
to the mysticism of this idea?

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:21.941
-I've been here in America
for a while

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.810
and I see people chasing
a lot of pretty crazy ideas.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.646
-[ Chuckles ] Yeah.

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:30.950
-Just because
somebody says it might be fun

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.186
and then
there's an insurrection.

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:34.520
So I...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.756
So I did...

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.158
Which I'm sure was
its own fun on the day.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.192
-I think on the day.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:42.095
We do forget that they were all

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:45.164
with a lot of like-minded people
having a grand old time.

00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:48.101
-And I suppose that doesn't
happen in this town yet.

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:49.535
But essentially what does happen

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:51.237
is that people change
the relationships.

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.871
People feel different
about themselves.

00:02:52.871 --> 00:02:55.408
And it does offer the premise
that even though the book

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:57.043
was written
before the pandemic,

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:59.712
it's got a reset kind of feel to
it where it's like if you're --

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:01.547
if you think you're not
doing life right, sure,

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:04.483
just give it another whirl.
-[ Chuckles ] Did you --

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:06.553
One thing in the clip,
we didn't fully see it,

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:08.387
but I know you got
a scooter in this.

00:03:08.387 --> 00:03:09.922
You got to ride
a scooter around.

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:11.357
Now, I would...

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.860
I would not --
I'd be worried about that

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.828
if I, as a performer,
had to get on a scooter.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:19.532
Are you somebody who's good
at those sort of things?

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:20.666
-I've been on a paddle board.

00:03:20.666 --> 00:03:21.667
If you've ever been
on a paddle board?

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.269
-I have. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:24.970
-Didn't go great, you know?

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.206
And the scooter was okay.

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:28.407
I don't have the center of a --

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:30.643
center of balance right
for a scooter,

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.045
but after a while,
just the gravity does it alone.

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:34.713
It's kind of --
It's hard to fall off.

00:03:34.713 --> 00:03:36.615
Just like,
as if you put a boulder

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:38.217
on a scooter and pushed it down.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:41.053
But, yeah,
I get some moves out.

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:42.655
It's -- It's very...
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:45.090
-It's very, very sexy.
-Oh, wow.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.061
-Yeah. I want people
to be prepared for that.

00:03:49.061 --> 00:03:50.395
-Do you think it's sexy
for everyone,

00:03:50.395 --> 00:03:52.365
or do you think you just have
the right kind of body

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:54.900
to be sexy on a scooter?
-I think that -- yes.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.137
Because I suppose
it's the -- it's the contrast

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:01.207
with the kind
of the narrowness of...

00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:02.308
That was a mistake.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.476
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.476 --> 00:04:06.445
I'm going to regret that
down the road.

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:07.713
-Eh, we're gonna...

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:10.216
We could edit it out
or leave it in, I feel like.

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:11.484
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:12.952
-But, yeah, I got
to do some scootering.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.720
I got to do some dancing,
which is fun.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.989
I get out all my skills, use six
of my faces.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:18.524
It's a whole thing.
-Did you --

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:21.193
When you were a kid,
did you do any of

00:04:21.193 --> 00:04:23.296
the sort of fortune teller stuff
like Magic Eight Ball

00:04:23.296 --> 00:04:25.297
or Ouija board or any of those?
-We did some Ouija boards.

00:04:25.297 --> 00:04:27.032
And this is kind of like that.

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:29.468
It's kind of like a Zoltar type
of machine, and then it can...

00:04:29.468 --> 00:04:31.370
But I don't know
if I would do it now.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.440
I feel a bit untethered now
that I would be like...

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.242
For example, it was my kid's
birthday a couple of weeks ago

00:04:37.242 --> 00:04:38.177
and we got a magician.

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:39.779
He's mad into magic.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:42.982
And the magician said
that his wife was a palm reader

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:44.617
and she could come
and do the adults

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.385
while everybody else was...

00:04:46.385 --> 00:04:48.187
And so she came.
Her name was Miss Zelda.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:49.588
I don't know if
it's her real name.

00:04:49.588 --> 00:04:52.824
-Yeah. And it seems pretty
fortuitous if that's it.

00:04:52.824 --> 00:04:54.493
-Maybe that's one
of those things

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:55.928
where it's a self-fulfilling
thing.

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:58.097
-Right. Like, if you're --
if you're a Zelda and you go --

00:04:58.097 --> 00:04:59.298
-She's not going to work in
insurance.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:01.100
-Right, exactly.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.236
-But anyway,
I didn't go to the lady

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:04.703
because I felt like I don't know

00:05:04.703 --> 00:05:06.705
if I could deal with the --
maybe she's right.

00:05:06.705 --> 00:05:07.973
I don't want her to be right.

00:05:07.973 --> 00:05:09.575
I just don't want
anything useful to happen.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.944
-I -- Yeah. I don't want
to know the future.

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:13.913
-Right.
-Yeah. Mostly because

00:05:13.913 --> 00:05:16.382
I feel like, good or bad,
if I get the information,

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.184
I would somehow screw it up.

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.118
-That's what I would do with
this machine where it's like,

00:05:20.118 --> 00:05:22.955
I think I do it just because
everybody was doing it.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:24.123
But I wouldn't want
anybody to know

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.658
what was on my card
because I don't

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.527
want next week for some [bleep]
to be like,

00:05:27.527 --> 00:05:28.895
"Are you an astronaut yet?"

00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:32.198
No, I'm not. I'm not.
I have napped.

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:34.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.268
-You play a high school teacher,
as you mentioned.

00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:38.104
Do you think that's something
that would fit your skill set?

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:40.540
Would you have liked that
as a path?

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:41.774
-I think it's an incredible job.

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:42.908
I don't know
if I have the patience for it.

00:05:42.908 --> 00:05:45.277
My sister is a teacher,
but I don't...

00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:47.413
I could --
I could be a professor.

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.982
-Oh, interesting. What is
your distinction of the two?

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:52.384
-Well, that's step two
that we haven't got to yet.

00:05:52.384 --> 00:05:54.987
But if there was anything
that I had some kind of in-depth

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:56.488
knowledge in, it would be --
it would be...

00:05:56.488 --> 00:05:58.591
I don't know if I could deal
with kids all the time.

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:00.225
-Yeah.
-They're so stupid.

00:06:00.225 --> 00:06:01.827
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.827 --> 00:06:03.428
By the way, I'm so...

00:06:03.428 --> 00:06:04.697
You did a wonderful show.

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:05.965
The first time
you were on the show

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:07.300
was for this wonderful show
called "Moone Boy."

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.035
-Yes.
-And I remember you were here,

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:10.336
and that was like 2014.

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:11.704
And I always remember
one of the things you said,

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:13.339
this was before I even had kids,
you said, you know,

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:14.574
the thing about kids
is they're so stupid.

00:06:14.574 --> 00:06:16.542
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.177
-And now I got them
and you got them.

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:19.578
And I want you to know,
I'm saying this

00:06:19.578 --> 00:06:22.014
with all authenticity,
I think about you all the time.

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:23.549
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:25.451
-Like when I --
Because I know it's you, too.

00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:26.785
Because when my kids
are misbehaving,

00:06:26.785 --> 00:06:28.620
I think in my head,
"You're so stupid."

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.590
And it's in an Irish accent.
-Good.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.025
I got to tell you,
a quick kid story

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:34.393
because this happened
a couple of weeks ago.

00:06:34.393 --> 00:06:35.762
So, I was -- I was -- I think

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:37.796
I was making breakfast
or something,

00:06:37.796 --> 00:06:40.032
and there's like a toilet
somewhere downstairs.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.234
And my kid was on --

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:43.503
my six-year-old
was sitting on the toilet.

00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:46.004
And so we just have
a conversation.

00:06:46.004 --> 00:06:47.940
And so he's just, "Daddy!

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.944
What if people
didn't have phones?"

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:55.381
And I was like, "Well, you know,

00:06:55.381 --> 00:06:57.783
people didn't
always have phones."

00:06:57.783 --> 00:06:59.552
And he said, "What?!"

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:02.554
And I said --
We're in different rooms.

00:07:02.554 --> 00:07:04.156
And I said,
"Well, when Daddy was young,

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:06.325
we didn't have
any mobile phones."

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:08.293
And then there was
this long pause.

00:07:08.293 --> 00:07:11.430
And then he said,
"I said bones!"

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:16.836
[ Laughter ]

00:07:16.836 --> 00:07:18.270
And what I realized, though,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.907
there was -- there was
a good eight seconds there

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:25.311
where he thought
when I was young there was...

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:28.647
[ Laughter ]

00:07:28.647 --> 00:07:30.549
-I also like...

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:33.085
-Like jellyfish people.
-Yeah. You had --

00:07:33.085 --> 00:07:35.587
You had sitting bones,
but no mobile bones.

00:07:35.587 --> 00:07:37.723
-[ Laughs ]
-You could stay in one place

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:39.324
with your bones,
but they wouldn't go anywhere.

00:07:39.324 --> 00:07:42.595
-Half man, half chair.
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:42.595 --> 00:07:44.030
What a delight
to have you back here.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.230
Thanks so much for joining us.
-Thank you.

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.366
-So great to see you.
-Chris O'Dowd, everybody.

00:07:47.366 --> 00:07:49.802
"The Big Door Prize" premieres
Wednesday on Apple TV+.

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:51.237
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

